      "text": "To find the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\), we need to start by identifying the general form of such an integer. An integer \\(N\\) that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\) can be written as:\n\\[ N = 22k + 15 \\]\nfor some integer \\(k\\). We want to find the largest 4-digit integer of this form, so we need to find the largest integer \\(k\\) such that \\(N\\) is still a 4-digit number. A 4-digit number ranges from 1000 to 9999. Therefore, we need:\n\\[ 1000 \\leq 22k + 15 \\leq 9999 \\]\nFirst, solve the inequality \\(1000 \\leq 22k + 15\\):\n\\[ 1000 - 15 \\leq 22k \\]\n\\[ 985 \\leq 22k \\]\n\\[ k \\geq \\frac{985}{22} \\]\n\\[ k \\geq 44.7727 \\]\nSince \\(k\\) must be an integer, we take the smallest integer greater than or equal to 44.7727, which is 45. So, the smallest possible value for \\(k\\) is 45. Next, we solve the inequality \\(22k + 15 \\leq 9999\\):\n\\[ 22k \\leq 9984 \\]\n\\[ k \\leq \\frac{9984}{22} \\]\n\\[ k \\leq 453.8182 \\]\nSince \\(k\\) must be an integer, we take the largest integer less than or equal to 453.8182, which is 453. So, the largest possible value for \\(k\\) is 453. Substituting \\(k = 453\\) into the expression for \\(N\\), we get:\n\\[ N = 22 \\cdot 453 + 15 \\]\n\\[ N = 9966 + 15 \\]\n\\[ N = 9981 \\]\nTherefore, the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\) is \\(\\boxed{9981}\\).",
