      "text": "To find the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\), we need to find an integer \\(n\\) such that:\n\n\\[ n \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22} \\]\n\nThis means that \\(n\\) can be written in the form:\n\n\\[ n = 22k + 15 \\]\n\nwhere \\(k\\) is an integer. We need \\(n\\) to be a 4-digit number, so we require:\n\n\\[ 1000 \\leq 22k + 15 \\leq 9999 \\]\n\nFirst, solve the inequality for the lower bound:\n\n\\[ 1000 \\leq 22k + 15 \\]\n\\[ 985 \\leq 22k \\]\n\\[ k \\geq \\frac{985}{22} \\]\n\\[ k \\geq 44.7727 \\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) must be an integer, we take the smallest integer greater than or equal to 44.7727, which is 45. So, the smallest possible value for \\(k\\) is 45.\n\nNext, solve the inequality for the upper bound:\n\n\\[ 22k + 15 \\leq 9999 \\]\n\\[ 22k \\leq 9984 \\]\n\\[ k \\leq \\frac{9984}{22} \\]\n\\[ k \\leq 453.8182 \\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) must be an integer, we take the largest integer less than or equal to 453.8182, which is 453. So, the largest possible value for \\(k\\) is 453.\n\nTherefore, the largest 4-digit integer of the form \\(22k + 15\\) is:\n\n\\[ n = 22 \\times 453 + 15 = 9966 + 15 = 9981 \\]\n\nThus, the largest 4-digit integer congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9981}\n\\]",
