      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the largest 4-digit number that is congruent to \\( 15 \\pmod{22} \\), we start by identifying the largest 4-digit number, which is 9999.\n\nWe need to find \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[\nx \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22}\n\\]\nThis means:\n\\[\nx = 22k + 15\n\\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nWe need \\( x \\) to be a 4-digit number, i.e., \\( 1000 \\leq x \\leq 9999 \\). Substituting \\( x = 22k + 15 \\), we get:\n\\[\n1000 \\leq 22k + 15 \\leq 9999\n\\]\n\nFirst, solve the inequality:\n\\[\n1000 \\leq 22k + 15\n\\]\nSubtract 15 from both sides:\n\\[\n985 \\leq 22k\n\\]\nDivide by 22:\n\\[\nk \\geq \\frac{985}{22} \\approx 44.7727\n\\]\nThus, the smallest integer \\( k \\) is 45.\n\nNext, solve the other inequality:\n\\[\n22k + 15 \\leq 9999\n\\]\nSubtract 15 from both sides:\n\\[\n22k \\leq 9984\n\\]\nDivide by 22:\n\\[\nk \\leq \\frac{9984}{22} \\approx 453.8182\n\\]\nThus, the largest integer \\( k \\) is 453.\n\nNow, we need to find the largest value of \\( x \\) within this range. Since \\( k = 453 \\), we substitute back to find \\( x \\):\n\\[\nx = 22 \\cdot 453 + 15\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\( 22 \\cdot 453 \\):\n\\[\n22 \\cdot 453 = 9966\n\\]\n\nAdd 15 to 9966:\n\\[\nx = 9966 + 15 = 9981\n\\]\n\nThus, the largest 4-digit number that is congruent to \\( 15 \\pmod{22} \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9981}\n\\]",
